# 4. Mutation run: each of 190 deliberate changes applied alone to a scratch copy of the client or the fake. Script: outputs/mutate-gitlab-client.sh  Output: outputs/mutation-run.txt
$ bash outputs/mutate-gitlab-client.sh <worktree>
mutations 190: caught 186, equivalent 4, not caught 0. A mutation counts as caught only when the mutated file still parses (node --check) and a test fails or hangs (cut by a 90 s timeout; 5 of them hang). A mutation that changes nothing (NOOP) or does not parse (INVALID) is reported as not caught.
The 4 equivalent mutants leave every test green on purpose, and each prints why: the client's own PRIVATE-TOKEN assignment overwrites a caller value (so the deny-list entry for it is a second layer); the final URL check and the character class of the path check refuse the same inputs (two independent layers); a rethrow in the catch is also done by the generic rethrow below it.
History: the first run of an earlier 82-mutation set left 7 survivors; a behavioural review (semantic_reviewer, advisory, not the pipeline's reviewer-agent accept) then found a confirmed path escape and two mutations whose mutated file did not parse but had been counted as caught. All of its confirmed findings are fixed (see the PR) and the harness now refuses an unparseable mutant.

