# 6. Facts the tests rest on, each checked
- Node's fetch follows redirects by default and delivers a custom header to the new origin. Run: outputs/redirect-check.mjs, output outputs/redirect-check.txt.
  redirect=follow: the second origin received the request with the PRIVATE-TOKEN header; redirect=manual: status 302 returned, zero requests to the second origin.
  (Node strips Authorization across origins but not other headers, so the client passes redirect: 'manual' and treats a 3xx as an error.)
- GitLab REST pagination (https://docs.gitlab.com/api/rest/): offset pagination takes page and per_page (max 100) and answers x-next-page; keyset pagination answers a
  Link header with rel="next"; the last page has no next. GitLab builds Link URLs from its configured external_url, so a client that talks to http://127.0.0.1 would see a
  Link on another origin and the client refuses it (fail closed). Offset pagination, which every lane of the plan uses, does not depend on Link.
- GitLab rate limiting answers 429 with a Retry-After header (GitLab support documentation on 429 errors); the client reads Retry-After in seconds or as an HTTP date and
  otherwise backs off exponentially.
